Generative AI Fundamentals

This fundamentals give an idea about how LLMs actually work, tokens, temperature, prompt engineering, and Azure's generative AI services.

What makes generative AI different

In previous days I learned about AI that analyzes (classifies, detects, extracts).
Today the concept is mainly about AI that creates — text, code, images, audio.

The engine: Large Language Models (LLMs).

How an LLM works
Training objective: predict the next token. That's it.
Do this billions of times across massive text data with a large enough model, and the model has to understand language, facts, and reasoning to predict well. Capability is a side effect of scale.

Tokens:
Not words, not characters — subword chunks. "unbelievable" → ["un", "believ", "able"] (3 tokens).
Rule of thumb: 1 token ≈ ¾ of a word

Why it matters as a developer:
APIs charge per token (input + output)
Models have a context window (max tokens they can "see" at once — like working memory)
Long log files won't fit in one call → need chunking strategy for the capstone

Temperature:
Controls randomness: 0.0 → focused/deterministic · 1.0+ → creative/varied
For log analysis: low temperature — you want consistent, accurate answers, not creative ones.

How generation works (the loop)

while (not done):
    convert tokens → embeddings (vectors)
    attention layers weigh relationships between all tokens
    output layer produces probability over vocabulary
    pick highest-probability token → append → repeat

Each token depends on all previous ones. Generation is autoregressive — one token at a time.

As a C# dev: a while loop calling a function that returns the next character, where the function has read every book ever written.

Prompt engineering — is the primary interface

We don't retrain the model; the prompt is how we control it.
Retrieve relevant docs at runtime, inject as context. Capstone plan: retrieve 3 similar past incidents from a vector store → inject → ask "given these, what is this error?"

